{"type":"meta","version":"0.1.1","title":"The Honeybee Colony","created":"2026-04-27T12:00:00+00:00","lastModified":"2026-04-27T12:00:00+00:00"}
{"type":"extension","id":"semantic-anchors","data":[{"word":"Waggle Dance","description":"A figure-8 dance performed by a forager on the vertical comb that encodes the direction and distance to a food source.","knowMoreUrl":null},{"word":"Queen Mandibular Pheromone","description":"A multi-component pheromone secreted by the queen that suppresses worker ovaries and signals her presence to the colony.","knowMoreUrl":null},{"word":"Brood","description":"The collective term for eggs, larvae, and pupae developing inside hexagonal comb cells.","knowMoreUrl":null},{"word":"Trophallaxis","description":"The exchange of food or other fluids mouth-to-mouth between bees — central to nutrition, signaling, and recognition.","knowMoreUrl":null},{"word":"Royal Jelly","description":"A protein-rich glandular secretion fed to all larvae briefly and to queen larvae exclusively, triggering queen development.","knowMoreUrl":null},{"word":"Propolis","description":"A resinous mixture bees collect from tree buds and use to seal and antiseptically coat hive cracks.","knowMoreUrl":null},{"word":"Apiary","description":"A managed location housing one or more honeybee colonies — backyard, rooftop, or commercial bee yard.","knowMoreUrl":null}]}
{"type":"layer","id":"f19c85fd-a61b-478a-b5f4-ab2587c5f414","level":0,"layout":"radial","nodes":[{"id":"4da0a3cd-336c-4d98-a121-28210e20bfd8","position":{"x":0,"y":0},"data":{"label":"The Honeybee Colony","summary":"A eusocial superorganism of one queen, 20 000–60 000 workers, and seasonal drones, organized by pheromone signaling and division of labor to produce honey, wax, and the next generation.","color":"#d2c09a"}}],"connections":[],"containers":[],"parentLayerId":null,"parentNodeId":null}
{"type":"layer","id":"8c0449f2-a13b-4ad6-9ae0-0d02d7072a19","level":1,"layout":"radial","nodes":[{"id":"9099d586-9457-4fb8-8768-ce8b8b0b2ef4","position":{"x":0,"y":-320},"data":{"label":"Castes & Roles","summary":"Three adult castes — queen, workers, drones — plus brood (eggs, larvae, pupae). Each caste has distinct anatomy, lifespan, and tasks.","color":"#d5bcfe"}},{"id":"8c94eede-7bed-47a7-a189-501465b18e93","position":{"x":304,"y":-99},"data":{"label":"Annual Cycle","summary":"Colonies expand from a winter cluster of ~10 000 to a summer peak of ~60 000, swarm in late spring, store honey through summer, then contract before winter.","color":"#b6eea7"}},{"id":"5b5495f4-a143-4392-9d22-91e997f46e45","position":{"x":188,"y":259},"data":{"label":"Hive Structure","summary":"Vertical stacks of wax comb: brood chamber below, honey supers above. Hexagonal cells store eggs, pollen, or capped honey.","color":"#fff699"}},{"id":"345ae949-a3b3-4e32-8c43-58a70f9ba8b8","position":{"x":-188,"y":259},"data":{"label":"Communication","summary":"Bees coordinate through the waggle dance, dozens of pheromones, trophallaxis (mouth-to-mouth feeding), and substrate vibrations on the comb.","color":"#ffbdfb"}},{"id":"bacb0410-5e2d-454f-b36c-9eea58353c27","position":{"x":-304,"y":-99},"data":{"label":"Foraging Economy","summary":"Foragers visit 50–100 flowers per trip, fly 2–5 km, and the colony processes ~30 kg of honey per year per healthy hive — a real annual energy budget.","color":"#fee7c3"}}],"connections":[],"containers":[],"parentLayerId":"f19c85fd-a61b-478a-b5f4-ab2587c5f414","parentNodeId":"4da0a3cd-336c-4d98-a121-28210e20bfd8"}
{"type":"layer","id":"f52e5869-83e6-4c1a-aae2-634469fe92ff","level":2,"layout":"radial","nodes":[{"id":"8d8b539a-b572-43d0-841b-4cbfa76b3f91","position":{"x":0,"y":-260},"data":{"label":"Queen (1 per hive)","summary":"Sole egg-layer; up to 2000 eggs/day in peak season; lives 3–5 years; emits Queen Mandibular Pheromone that signals colony cohesion.","color":"#d5bcfe"}},{"id":"0dab448d-444a-49a0-a8c5-6a7770783545","position":{"x":260,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Workers (20–60k)","summary":"Sterile females; perform every non-laying task; 6-week summer lifespan, 6-month winter lifespan; size of the colony rises and falls with their numbers.","color":"#d5bcfe"}},{"id":"1f51161a-2462-4c66-bc9c-2f476a728b11","position":{"x":0,"y":260},"data":{"label":"Drones (~hundreds)","summary":"Males; produced only in late spring/summer; only function is mating flights at 10–20 m altitude; evicted before winter.","color":"#d5bcfe"}},{"id":"4fdbeb69-19d9-4d53-bafd-8f8bc82e8dac","position":{"x":-260,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Brood","summary":"Eggs (3 days) → larvae (6 days, fed by nurses) → capped pupae (12 days). Total: 21 days for workers, 16 for queens, 24 for drones.","color":"#d5bcfe"}}],"connections":[],"containers":[{"id":"bbdb2f8f-08cf-4011-a7ed-0efc54da5c98","nodes":["8d8b539a-b572-43d0-841b-4cbfa76b3f91","1f51161a-2462-4c66-bc9c-2f476a728b11","4fdbeb69-19d9-4d53-bafd-8f8bc82e8dac"],"data":{"label":"Reproductive caste","formation":"radial","style":"dashed"}},{"id":"fe7650f0-25af-4297-adcc-e67b1a6b40bb","nodes":["0dab448d-444a-49a0-a8c5-6a7770783545"],"data":{"label":"Sterile labor","formation":"radial","style":"none"}}],"parentLayerId":"8c0449f2-a13b-4ad6-9ae0-0d02d7072a19","parentNodeId":"9099d586-9457-4fb8-8768-ce8b8b0b2ef4"}
{"type":"layer","id":"24764dcd-fb5d-4330-84aa-36b92ece6f5d","level":2,"layout":"linear-horizontal","nodes":[{"id":"1aefb3d1-e7a2-4e64-8ab0-6769daf8b3c1","position":{"x":-750,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Late-Winter Cluster","summary":"Cluster of ~10 000 maintains a 35 °C core by shivering; consumes ~1 kg honey/week; queen begins slow egg-laying.","color":"#b6eea7"}},{"id":"d112826c-7332-4180-b71d-f60807d867c9","position":{"x":-500,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Spring Buildup","summary":"First pollen flow triggers nurse-bee production; queen lays 1500–2000 eggs/day; population doubles in 6 weeks.","color":"#b6eea7"}},{"id":"212f1174-99be-470e-be6b-6859daefe8d7","position":{"x":-250,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Swarm Season","summary":"Crowded May–June hives raise a new queen; old queen leaves with ~half the workers — natural colony reproduction.","color":"#b6eea7"}},{"id":"ff52e5ac-fdfd-4045-8156-cff732b01753","position":{"x":0,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Honey Flow","summary":"Major nectar sources bloom; foragers collect 2–5 kg/day on peak days; bees fan wings to evaporate water to <18 %.","color":"#b6eea7"}},{"id":"a1b1ff63-1e19-4f87-bb19-b8f0b9c8ee2b","position":{"x":250,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Summer Peak","summary":"Colony reaches 50 000–60 000 bees; honey supers fill; queen's egg-laying tapers as the nectar flow wanes.","color":"#b6eea7"}},{"id":"70030aeb-d1d5-4d25-a819-615c72908a8b","position":{"x":500,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Fall Preparation","summary":"Workers expel drones; propolize cracks; queen reduces laying; cluster size targets 30 000 winter bees.","color":"#b6eea7"}},{"id":"f781143c-1722-4c11-be7c-03454d43800e","position":{"x":750,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Winter Cluster (35 °C core)","summary":"Bees form a tight ball; outer mantle bees rotate inward to warm; cluster moves slowly across the comb to access stored honey.","color":"#b6eea7"}}],"connections":[{"id":"b65fd838-c028-4a75-8c0b-5ef1220daf82","from":"1aefb3d1-e7a2-4e64-8ab0-6769daf8b3c1","to":"d112826c-7332-4180-b71d-f60807d867c9","data":{"label":"precedes","style":"solid"}},{"id":"54083a9d-aff7-48de-9476-f77c86518d9f","from":"d112826c-7332-4180-b71d-f60807d867c9","to":"212f1174-99be-470e-be6b-6859daefe8d7","data":{"label":"precedes","style":"solid"}},{"id":"4060582f-c70c-4c0f-96e6-07e09a9ecf79","from":"212f1174-99be-470e-be6b-6859daefe8d7","to":"ff52e5ac-fdfd-4045-8156-cff732b01753","data":{"label":"precedes","style":"solid"}},{"id":"3ba65446-c632-4184-8f3a-e0a840dfd4f0","from":"ff52e5ac-fdfd-4045-8156-cff732b01753","to":"a1b1ff63-1e19-4f87-bb19-b8f0b9c8ee2b","data":{"label":"precedes","style":"solid"}},{"id":"b0949e97-c6ef-46ef-9413-4d1a9a988f14","from":"a1b1ff63-1e19-4f87-bb19-b8f0b9c8ee2b","to":"70030aeb-d1d5-4d25-a819-615c72908a8b","data":{"label":"precedes","style":"solid"}},{"id":"a717a458-d166-472c-ba10-d7552880ebcc","from":"70030aeb-d1d5-4d25-a819-615c72908a8b","to":"f781143c-1722-4c11-be7c-03454d43800e","data":{"label":"precedes","style":"solid"}},{"id":"40b599fc-4099-4ad5-89ab-24000a1879a8","from":"ff52e5ac-fdfd-4045-8156-cff732b01753","to":"70030aeb-d1d5-4d25-a819-615c72908a8b","data":{"label":"triggers","style":"dashed"}}],"containers":[{"id":"c897479f-ae73-49d4-844c-2cdc7f64fe9f","nodes":["d112826c-7332-4180-b71d-f60807d867c9","212f1174-99be-470e-be6b-6859daefe8d7","ff52e5ac-fdfd-4045-8156-cff732b01753","a1b1ff63-1e19-4f87-bb19-b8f0b9c8ee2b"],"data":{"label":"Active foraging","formation":"linear-horizontal","style":"dashed"}},{"id":"f3ee1167-83d5-41ab-a099-90dd776685a2","nodes":["1aefb3d1-e7a2-4e64-8ab0-6769daf8b3c1","f781143c-1722-4c11-be7c-03454d43800e"],"data":{"label":"Survival mode","formation":"linear-horizontal","style":"none"}}],"parentLayerId":"8c0449f2-a13b-4ad6-9ae0-0d02d7072a19","parentNodeId":"8c94eede-7bed-47a7-a189-501465b18e93"}
{"type":"layer","id":"a4fd2518-271d-43a0-88df-df7f975110cd","level":2,"layout":"radial","nodes":[{"id":"0bbc50a8-2fe2-48b4-8266-3f89d4b85a0e","position":{"x":0,"y":-260},"data":{"label":"Brood Chamber","summary":"Lower box; holds the queen and developing brood; comb cells ~5.3 mm wide for workers, 6.9 mm for drones.","color":"#fff699"}},{"id":"a5091a6a-b570-4367-85db-f5a426139b3d","position":{"x":260,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Honey Super","summary":"Upper boxes; capped honey storage; queen excluder grid keeps her below — only workers fit through.","color":"#fff699"}},{"id":"65597e81-5728-449f-b269-c7c45c674b00","position":{"x":0,"y":260},"data":{"label":"Hexagonal Comb Cells","summary":"Six-sided wax cells use the least wax per unit volume — a geometric efficiency proof bees solved 100 M years ago.","color":"#fff699"}},{"id":"7a0063ba-1005-44cd-bde1-7f6d7d990c72","position":{"x":-260,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Entrance & Landing Board","summary":"Single defended entrance; guard bees inspect arrivals via pheromone signature; foragers land here and dance inside.","color":"#fff699"}}],"connections":[],"containers":[{"id":"7120503b-a4e6-44ff-8f47-f332b62689a0","nodes":["0bbc50a8-2fe2-48b4-8266-3f89d4b85a0e","a5091a6a-b570-4367-85db-f5a426139b3d","65597e81-5728-449f-b269-c7c45c674b00"],"data":{"label":"Wax architecture","formation":"radial","style":"dashed"}}],"parentLayerId":"8c0449f2-a13b-4ad6-9ae0-0d02d7072a19","parentNodeId":"5b5495f4-a143-4392-9d22-91e997f46e45"}
{"type":"layer","id":"7dbdfc1f-3f16-4ff0-b017-14c99551b048","level":2,"layout":"radial","nodes":[{"id":"9c6b4126-d676-4b9a-8a6b-209d525398ea","position":{"x":0,"y":-260},"data":{"label":"Waggle Dance","summary":"Figure-8 dance on the vertical comb communicates direction (relative to the sun) and distance (waggle-run duration) of a food source.","color":"#ffbdfb"}},{"id":"6471979d-8324-4950-9143-0e7f5cc1e0d6","position":{"x":260,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Pheromones (QMP)","summary":"Queen Mandibular Pheromone + dozens of others: alarm, brood, Nasonov (homing), drone congregation. Chemistry rules colony state.","color":"#ffbdfb"}},{"id":"4e519b5e-03f4-452c-af69-4844426829f9","position":{"x":0,"y":260},"data":{"label":"Trophallaxis","summary":"Mouth-to-mouth food sharing distributes nutrition, pheromones, and microbiome between bees — also a recognition behavior.","color":"#ffbdfb"}},{"id":"569cae11-22a6-4e90-ab97-485b1b0c8afb","position":{"x":-260,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Vibration Signals","summary":"Buzz runs, dorsoventral vibrations, and stop signals modulate dance recruitment and arouse foragers.","color":"#ffbdfb"}}],"connections":[{"id":"bd0c8a22-f9fe-4c79-b38b-17d059e0311b","from":"569cae11-22a6-4e90-ab97-485b1b0c8afb","to":"9c6b4126-d676-4b9a-8a6b-209d525398ea","data":{"label":"amplifies","style":"dashed"}}],"containers":[],"parentLayerId":"8c0449f2-a13b-4ad6-9ae0-0d02d7072a19","parentNodeId":"345ae949-a3b3-4e32-8c43-58a70f9ba8b8"}
{"type":"layer","id":"2dd6c921-7620-4c16-babc-d6f9709a4aa1","level":3,"layout":"radial","nodes":[{"id":"fc2d574a-ce88-4be6-8bb5-8ab7fb10cd57","position":{"x":0,"y":-260},"data":{"label":"Egg-Laying 1500–2000/day","summary":"At peak laying she places one egg per minute, each in a clean cell prepared by workers — body weight of eggs/day exceeds her own.","color":"#faada5"}},{"id":"874ec5b4-3c53-4800-b4d9-3562c1efd619","position":{"x":260,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Queen Mandibular Pheromone","summary":"5-component pheromone broadcast by attendant workers via grooming — suppresses worker ovaries and queen-cell building.","color":"#faada5"}},{"id":"bb337549-426d-48a7-8afe-ffe408df7705","position":{"x":0,"y":260},"data":{"label":"Mating Flights at 10–20 m","summary":"Virgin queens mate with 10–20 drones at high-altitude congregation areas over 1–2 days, storing sperm for life.","color":"#faada5"}},{"id":"d2ffb741-2d71-4d7e-86de-c6c2f0e7e264","position":{"x":-260,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Lifespan 3–5 years","summary":"10–20× longer than a worker; longevity tied to royal-jelly nutrition and reduced oxidative stress in the protected hive.","color":"#faada5"}}],"connections":[],"containers":[],"parentLayerId":"f52e5869-83e6-4c1a-aae2-634469fe92ff","parentNodeId":"8d8b539a-b572-43d0-841b-4cbfa76b3f91"}
{"type":"layer","id":"a253b0d7-2be1-459b-bc38-8c18440dc2d8","level":3,"layout":"linear-vertical","nodes":[{"id":"e2f52c57-311c-480b-8ef4-4a03f9c70afc","position":{"x":0,"y":-340},"data":{"label":"Cell Cleaner (day 1–3)","summary":"Newly emerged adults polish empty cells with saliva and wax to prepare them for the queen's next egg.","color":"#ffcfa1"}},{"id":"a07a4288-a91a-47bd-880f-235902d111cc","position":{"x":0,"y":-170},"data":{"label":"Nurse Bee (day 4–12)","summary":"Hypopharyngeal glands produce royal jelly; nurses feed each larva up to 1300 times per day.","color":"#e9ed98"}},{"id":"2a880d8b-d8ad-4c88-ad1e-19914b77af34","position":{"x":0,"y":0},"data":{"label":"House Bee (day 13–18)","summary":"Receives nectar from foragers, processes it into honey, builds and repairs comb, and removes debris.","color":"#ffcfa1"}},{"id":"1d3b90a5-b408-474a-8d16-f6d5a5678042","position":{"x":0,"y":170},"data":{"label":"Guard Bee (day 19–22)","summary":"Stations at the entrance; inspects incoming bees by pheromone signature; first to release alarm pheromone if attacked.","color":"#e9ed98"}},{"id":"877953de-1ee9-4b23-8728-0b83afb29efa","position":{"x":0,"y":340},"data":{"label":"Forager (day 23+)","summary":"Final third of life: flies up to 5 km per trip collecting nectar, pollen, propolis, or water; wears down wings until death.","color":"#ffcfa1"}}],"connections":[{"id":"bffad478-51a9-4c8a-93c1-0befa73d1251","from":"e2f52c57-311c-480b-8ef4-4a03f9c70afc","to":"a07a4288-a91a-47bd-880f-235902d111cc","data":{"label":"graduates to","style":"solid"}},{"id":"ed0a1d3b-439d-451d-b57d-7db8df35ff1b","from":"a07a4288-a91a-47bd-880f-235902d111cc","to":"2a880d8b-d8ad-4c88-ad1e-19914b77af34","data":{"label":"graduates to","style":"solid"}},{"id":"dcdf6fea-3f7c-499a-b2be-e2a8d00900b9","from":"2a880d8b-d8ad-4c88-ad1e-19914b77af34","to":"1d3b90a5-b408-474a-8d16-f6d5a5678042","data":{"label":"graduates to","style":"solid"}},{"id":"d32a78c2-fb18-4b19-8926-cd68049e5735","from":"1d3b90a5-b408-474a-8d16-f6d5a5678042","to":"877953de-1ee9-4b23-8728-0b83afb29efa","data":{"label":"graduates to","style":"solid"}},{"id":"13381968-5125-43aa-bf7f-bcc5a3a17bf0","from":"a07a4288-a91a-47bd-880f-235902d111cc","to":"e2f52c57-311c-480b-8ef4-4a03f9c70afc","data":{"label":"groomed by","style":"dashed"}}],"containers":[{"id":"f4f87967-a7df-4755-a4c4-d68f14d1cb55","nodes":["e2f52c57-311c-480b-8ef4-4a03f9c70afc","a07a4288-a91a-47bd-880f-235902d111cc","2a880d8b-d8ad-4c88-ad1e-19914b77af34"],"data":{"label":"In-hive duties","formation":"linear-vertical","style":"dashed"}}],"parentLayerId":"f52e5869-83e6-4c1a-aae2-634469fe92ff","parentNodeId":"0dab448d-444a-49a0-a8c5-6a7770783545"}
{"type":"layer","id":"aca4a6dd-7984-4a90-a38a-8c802b62f82a","level":3,"layout":"radial","nodes":[{"id":"30160274-a21c-4f6b-8d3c-b2ee81940756","position":{"x":0,"y":-260},"data":{"label":"Direction (vs. sun)","summary":"Angle of the waggle run on the vertical comb, measured off vertical, equals the angle to the food source measured off the sun azimuth.","color":"#a6f5d8"}},{"id":"2b51427d-781f-412d-bdea-383f28684a94","position":{"x":260,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Distance (waggle duration)","summary":"Each second of waggling encodes ~1 km of distance — calibrated to the species and verified by hundreds of experiments since von Frisch.","color":"#a6f5d8"}},{"id":"f20325d5-1e06-4696-b72c-decc7310a49c","position":{"x":0,"y":260},"data":{"label":"Quality (vigor)","summary":"More excited, repeated dances signal richer food sources; observers preferentially follow vigorous dancers.","color":"#a6f5d8"}},{"id":"4ac115bd-4659-4c2a-8047-d0ad5bd56aff","position":{"x":-260,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Recruitment (followers depart)","summary":"Followers trail the dancer for a few cycles, sample regurgitated nectar, then fly out — typically 5–20 recruits per successful dance.","color":"#a6f5d8"}}],"connections":[],"containers":[],"parentLayerId":"7dbdfc1f-3f16-4ff0-b017-14c99551b048","parentNodeId":"9c6b4126-d676-4b9a-8a6b-209d525398ea"}
